    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d371">

                <pb facs="0887" n="852" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v32/pg852"/>

                <head>371. Memorandum From Secretary of State Rusk to President Johnson<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d371-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-02-06">February 6, 1964</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Visit of British Prime Minister Home; British Guiana</item>
                </list>
                <p>I recommend you make the following points to Sir Alec Home regarding British
                    Guiana: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>You are as concerned as President Kennedy over British Guiana.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>Emergence of another Communist state in this hemisphere cannot be
                            accepted; there is grave risk of Jaganʼs establishing a Castro-type
                            regime should he attain independence.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Prime Minister Macmillan and President Kennedy agreed that British
                            Guiana should not become independent under Jagan<anchor n="2"
                                xml:id="d371-f2"/> and that a change of government must be
                            sought.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item> Jagan must be defeated in the next election.</item>
                        <label>5.</label>
                        <item>Suspension of the constitution and imposition of direct rule would
                            help defeat Jagan.</item>
                        <label>6.</label>
                        <item>Direct British control over internal security, strengthening the
                            police, and a broad interpretation of the powers reserved to the UK in
                            foreign affairs to prevent entry of personnel and funds from Cuba would
                            help overcome the atmosphere of intimidation Jagan is trying to
                            create.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p> Sir Alec will probably (1) confirm the Macmillan/Kennedy understanding; (2)
                    endorse the importance of assuring Jaganʼs defeat; <pb facs="0888" n="853"
                        xml:id="pg853"/> (3) question the feasibility of a resumption of direct UK
                    rule unless the grounds can be publicly shown to be fully justified.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Dean Rusk</hi>
                        <anchor n="3" xml:id="d371-f3"/>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d371-a1.xml" xpointer="d371-a1"/>
            </div>
